      <h2>About Fukuchani Ruins</h2>
      <div>Fukuchani ruins on Zanzibar's northwest coast reveal the archaeological remains of a 16th-century Portuguese settlement and earlier Shirazi trading post dating back to the 12th century. Coral stone foundations, mosque remnants, and pottery fragments tell the story of Zanzibar's layered maritime history and Swahili coast trade networks. The site is accessible via a scenic beach walk from Kendwa or Nungwi, passing traditional fishing villages. A quiet, off-the-beaten-path historical excursion.</div>
